  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers and thunderstorms through the\nweekend. A few severe storms are possible, with the best chance\nbeing Sunday. Very heavy downpours with any storms could lead \nto localized flooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nShortwave swinging through Saturday, bringing a potential for\nshowers. The best chance for storms on Saturday will be Downeast\nalong the sea breeze boundary through the afternoon, with the\nthreat for storms shifting northwards Saturday evening as the\nnext low pressure system to the northwest pushes eastward, with\na cold front approaching on Sunday. There is some instability \nahead of the cold front, as moisture and instability funnels \ninto the region this weekend. Models continue to align with \n1,500 - 2,000 J/kg of CAPE and and plenty of precipitation. \nSoundings are very moist, with classic long and skinny CAPE \nthroughout most of northern and eastern Maine. PWAT values \naround 1.5-1.75\u201d, which is roughly in the 90th percentile for \nPWATs based on sounding archives. Storm motions could be slow \nwith these storms, and if any slow moving storms do develop with\nheavier showers, there could be localized flooding. Three hour \nflash flood guidance is roughly 1.5 - 2\u201d in the north, and 2-3\u201d \nDowneast. Currently, looking at maybe \u00bd an inch of rain, but \nareas with convection could so higher totals. Shear is weak, \nhowever, and cold front does not seem to be very strong.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Scattered rain showers and thunderstorms early\nnext week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nRounds of shortwaves move through the north early next week,\nbringing a chance for rain showers and some thunderstorms.\nThunder possible in the north on Monday, and areawide Tuesday.\nEnvironment does not look too impressive for strong \nthunderstorms, so could just be some brief isolated storms below\nsevere criteria.",
